title The fold that met another fold
prompt Approve the recommendations -- take the ledger fold, then the roster fan-out meter.
think I RECOMMENDED THIS AND THE TREE HAD ALREADY RULED IT THE WRONG INSTRUMENT. Reading construction/archive/20260917-071327_reds-pin-bound-raise-account.md before acting turned the recommendation over: its closing sentence is a bound is the wrong instrument for a page whose rows do not close, and the cure is closing rows rather than carrying them. It forecast its own failure and was right twice -- raised 20260915 for 24 rows, over bound again within two days at pin_foldable_rows=0.
think So I did the fold and said out loud what it buys. Measured rather than assumed: 21 rows on the pin, NINETEEN read OPEN, two read CLOSED. 5,445 bytes was the whole foldable population. The pin goes 65,533 to 60,082, which is roughly two rows of room.
think The timing convention was checked rather than guessed. REDS-whose-else-is-this-rows-811.md was folded the hour it was written, for this exact reason, and calls itself the second shelf to say so. Mine is the third, and the shelf says which number it is so the pattern stays countable.
think %817 was to travel beside mine, and A PEER FOLDED IT FIRST while this lap ran. The two folds met as a rebase conflict on the pin, where upstream held three things my side had dropped: a peer's newly booked row at stamp 20260917.185557, my own %818, and a pointer line naming the peer's %817 shelf. Resolved BY HAND rather than by either side winning -- take upstream whole, remove only my %818 -- which is %812's own lesson one file over: a rebase settling a ledger in favour of one side is ordinary git behaviour and silently lossy.
think So the shelf carries ONE row rather than two, and it says why on its own face. A row must stand in exactly one file (%536), and shipping my two-row shelf beside the peer's would have put %817 in two -- a red booked by the fold meant to tidy.
obs THE PEER BOOKED THE RED I MET. The row at 20260917.185557 is the operator card sitting 482 bytes over its bound and blocking every commit on the pier -- the wall that stopped this very lap an hour ago. It is cited by stamp rather than number, correctly, since the spine has yet to bind it.
obs THE REAL CONDITION IS UNCHANGED AND IT IS NOT A LEDGER PROBLEM. Nineteen open rows is nineteen defects this fleet has published and not closed. No fold, bound raise, or shelf touches that. The two instruments I have now recommended -- this fold and the next bound conversation -- are both downstream of it.
obs A commit body was refused three times for mechanism words, each refusal correct, and the third landed once the sentence named the file, the constant and the directory. The wall reads vocabulary rather than comprehension and said so; what it bought here was a body that names where the bytes went.
loom pin=construction/REDS.md bytes_before=65533 bytes_after=61978 bound=65536 headroom_after=3558
loom rows_on_pin=21 rows_open=19 rows_closed=2 rows_folded_here=1 rows_folded_by_peer=1 conflicts_resolved_by_hand=1
rota none -- an approved maintenance lap, no rota
file construction/archive/REDS-the-reading-a-guard-could-not-take-rows-818.md the shelf, carrying one row whole and naming both what the fold does not fix and why it holds one row rather than two
file construction/REDS.md my CLOSED row lifts off the pin; the peer's new row, their %817 pointer, every OPEN row and the census headline all stand
file construction/archive/REDS-fold-recital.md one recital line records the fold, its stamp and its honest limit
scope one approved fold, claimed and proven
status sent -- both remotes
recommend keep-going -- the fan-out meter is next; nineteen open rows stand behind every instrument here, and two ships folding one row in one hour is its own small reading about a fleet this size.
